-- hypermath links.hm
-- Named non-sequential connections between forms across any layer.
-- The ordinal_reflection operation: the unifying primitive beneath
-- commutativity, subtraction, and the wrap operation W.
--
-- Presupposes:
--   link axioms.hm -> ground   [□ primitive]
--   link axioms.hm -> similar  [~~ relation]
--   link axioms.hm -> congruent [=~ relation]
--   link axioms.hm -> simulation [== relation]
--   link axioms.hm -> ax-box   [two applications return to ~~-class]
--   link axioms.hm -> definitive-expansion [+ operator]
--   link axioms.hm -> additionally-is-commutative
--   link casting.hm -> cast-totality
--   link casting.hm -> form-status-from-casting
--   link casting.hm -> conditionality-is-a-gate
--   link casting.hm -> ordinatic-ordering
--   link casting.hm -> ordinal_reflection          [Form -> Form -> Relation]
--   link casting.hm -> ordinal_reflection-is-symmetric
--
-- Note: "link" appears in the presupposes header as the formal replacement
-- for bulk "presupposes: file" imports. Each named dependency is exact.
-- The kernel uses these link declarations to trace step-level provenance.
--
-- Status: FORM for link definition, Möbius validity, ordinal_reflection,
--         commutativity derived from ordinal_reflection.
--         FRAME for W-as-ordinal_reflection (requires subtraction.hm).

-- ================================================================
-- § 1. Etymology — Why "link"
-- ================================================================
--
-- link (n.) — Old English hlence: a ring in a chain.
-- A ring is not the chain. It is the connector between two parts
-- of the chain that are not adjacent to each other.
-- A link names where continuation resumes when the next step
-- is not the immediately following form.
--
-- Three connectivity forms now exist in hypermath_form:
--
--   +            inter-layer, sequential, ordered.
--                A's end joins to B's beginning. Adjacent layers only.
--                b expands a from a's end. Non-commutative.
--
--   additionally intra-layer, unordered, co-present.
--                Two forms at the same derivation level.
--                Commutative: order of statement does not affect closure.
--
--   link         any-layer, named, non-sequential.
--                Names a path of continuation between two forms
--                that are not adjacent in the derivation chain.
--                A ring in the chain: it connects without being
--                either form it connects.
--
-- The vocabulary DAG is a link structure:
--   "unfair" link-> "fair" (via un- inversion)
--   "fair" link-> Proto-Germanic *fagraz (via etymological chain)
--   The layers of characterization ARE a link graph.
-- Without links, encoding the DAG would require flattening all
-- layers into one file — a RULE 8 violation.

-- ================================================================
-- § 2. Link as a Form
-- ================================================================

-- A link L is a form with three components:
--   source      — the form L departs from
--   target      — the form L arrives at
--   direction   — forward (source -> target) or return (target -> source)
--
-- link :: Form -> Form -> Direction -> Form
--
-- direction is a two-valued form:
--   forward-direction: the link traversed from source to target
--   return-direction:  the link traversed from target to source
--
-- These are not Boolean. They are orientation labels derived from
-- the Möbius structure defined in § 3.

-- ================================================================
-- § 3. Möbius Validity — The One-Sided Constraint
-- ================================================================
--
-- Etymology of "Möbius":
--   Named for August Möbius, who described the one-sided surface.
--   "One-sided" is the structural content: a surface with one side
--   is a surface where inside and outside are the same surface,
--   experienced from different orientations.
--   In a two-sided surface, orientation is fixed at each point.
--   In a Möbius surface, orientation flips continuously as you traverse.
--   One traversal: return to starting position, orientation flipped.
--   Two traversals: return to starting position, orientation restored.
--   This is ax-box at the topological level: □(□(x)) ~~ x.
--
-- A link L is Möbius-valid if these four conditions hold:

derive möbius-validity as FORM:
    -- Definitions:
    -- traverse-forward(L, A):  follow L from source A to target B.
    -- traverse-return(L, B):   follow L from target B back toward A.
    --                          Returns A' (possibly different orientation from A).

    step 1: FORWARD CHANGES POSITION.
            traverse-forward(L, A) -> B
            where B != A at the == level.
            A link that returns to the exact == starting point on the
            forward traversal is not a link — it is a no-op.
            Condition: traverse-forward(L, A) =~-or-above A is not required.
            It is only required that the forward traversal moves.

    step 2: RETURN REACHES ~~ BUT NOT ==.
            traverse-return(L, B) -> A'
            where A' ~~ A  additionally  not(A' == A).
            One full Möbius traversal returns to the ~~-class of origin
            but with flipped orientation: the path was taken, structure changed,
            similarity preserved, identity not restored.
            This is the Möbius condition directly: one loop, one orientation flip.

    step 3: TWO TRAVERSALS RESTORE ~~ CLASS (ax-box at link level).
            traverse-forward(traverse-return(L, traverse-forward(L, A)), L)
            ~~ A.
            Two full traversals return to the ~~-class of A.
            This is ax-box instanced at the link level.
            The link structure inherits the closure property of □.

    step 4: FORWARD AND RETURN ARE DISTINCT (non-commutativity preserved).
            traverse-forward(L, A) !=~~ traverse-return(L, A).
            The forward and return paths are not similar to each other.
            They take different routes through the same surface.
            If they were similar, the link would be two-sided (ring structure)
            and commutativity would be the default rather than the earned case.

    close: relation(=~)
    -- Möbius-validity is fully derived from ax-box and the one-sided surface structure.
    -- FORM at =~: all continuation futures of a Möbius-valid link are determined.

-- ================================================================
-- § 4. ordinal_reflection — Imported from casting.hm
-- ================================================================
--
-- ordinal_reflection :: Form -> Form -> Relation
-- Defined in casting.hm § 6 (link casting.hm -> ordinal_reflection).
--
-- ordinal_reflection(A, A') measures the ordinal distance between
-- the starting form A and the return form A' after one full cycle.
-- It is defined on Forms directly — not on Links.
-- Links.hm applies it by supplying the specific Form pairs that
-- arise from link traversals: A (start) and A' (return after traverse).
--
-- Why it belongs in casting.hm and not here:
-- casting.hm is the layer that closes on: what □ does to Forms
-- and how far the return lands. ordinal_reflection is intrinsic
-- to the cast operation — it measures the cast's return residue.
-- links.hm is the layer that introduces the Link type and uses
-- ordinal_reflection to define what makes a Link valid (Möbius).
-- The + boundary between casting.hm and links.hm is load-bearing:
-- Möbius validity depends on ordinal_reflection being FORM first.
-- Co-presence (additionally) of ordinal_reflection and Möbius validity
-- in the same layer would remove that ordering guarantee — the layer
-- would have no closed end to expand from.

-- ================================================================
-- § 5. Commutativity Derived from ordinal_reflection
-- ================================================================
--
-- Commutativity is not presupposed from classical algebra.
-- It is the specific case where ordinal_reflection returns ==.

derive commutativity-from-ordinal_reflection as FORM:
    step 1: two link traversals are commutative iff
            traverse-forward(L1, A) followed by traverse-forward(L2, B)
            returns to the exact == starting position A.
    step 2: the return form after both traversals is A'.
            ordinal_reflection(A, A') measures the ordinal distance.
            (ordinal_reflection :: Form -> Form -> Relation, casting.hm § 6)
    step 3: ordinal_reflection(A, A') closes at ==
            iff A' is at the same ordinal position as A by the same path.
            Zero ordinal distance = same position, same path = commutativity.
    step 4: therefore: L1 and L2 are commutative
            iff ordinal_reflection(A, traverse-return-form(L1,L2,A)) closes at ==.
    step 5: additionally is already commutative (axioms.hm additionally-is-commutative).
            Confirming: for any A, additionally co-presence returns the same A.
            ordinal_reflection(A, A) == ==  (zero distance, same position). HOLDS.
    step 6: + is non-commutative (axioms.hm definitive-expansion).
            Confirming: A = quanta_1, L1 = link to ordinal_limit.
            traverse-forward(L1, quanta_1) -> ordinal_limit (absorbed).
            traverse-forward(L2, ordinal_limit) -> ordinal_limit + quanta_1.
            ordinal_reflection(quanta_1, ordinal_limit + quanta_1) closes at ~~.
            Different ordinal positions. Non-commutative. HOLDS.
    close: relation(==)
    -- Commutativity is derived. FORM at ==.
    -- Non-commutativity is the default (Möbius). Commutativity is the earned case.

-- ================================================================
-- § 6. ordinal_reflection Unifies Three Derived Concepts
-- ================================================================
--
-- ordinal_reflection is not one operation among many.
-- It is the single operation from which three major derived concepts
-- in the system follow as specific evaluation contexts.

-- (A) SUBTRACTION as ordinal_reflection-as-distance
--
-- α ⊖ β is the ordinal distance measured by ordinal_reflection(β, α):
-- the Form β and the Form α, asking: how far apart are they ordinally?
-- The gap is the value of the subtraction.
-- This is partial (cond(β <= α, ...)) because ordinal_reflection
-- only returns a meaningful gap-form when β has not passed α.
-- FRAME: subtraction.hm writes the full derive block with existence proof.

-- (B) COMMUTATIVITY as ordinal_reflection closing at ==
-- Already derived in § 5 above. FORM.

-- (C) W (the wrap operation) as ordinal_reflection at the ω boundary
--
-- W maps transfinite ordinal forms to their canonical finite representatives.
-- A Möbius traversal through ordinal_limit (ω) and back:
--   traverse-forward(link-to-ω, A) reaches the limit form.
--   traverse-return(link-from-ω, ω) — the return traversal through the limit.
--   The ordinal_reflection of this traversal collapses to the canonical
--   finite form in the ~~-class of the starting form.
--
-- This is why W(1 + 2 + 3 + ...) = -1/12:
--   The triangular ordimate T(ω) is the target of traverse-forward.
--   The return traversal through ω produces the finite form in ~~-class.
--   ordinal_reflection measures zero ordinal distance at the ~~ level
--   between T(ω) and -1/12 (they are in the same ~~-class).
--   -1/12 is the canonical finite representative because it is the
--   unique finite form whose ~~-class intersects T(ω)'s ~~-class
--   under the Möbius return through ordinal_limit.
--
-- W is ordinal_reflection evaluated at the ordinal_limit boundary.
-- FRAME: full derivation requires subtraction.hm to close the
--        finite-layer existence proof first, then ordinatic-wrap.hm
--        applies ordinal_reflection at the limit.

-- ================================================================
-- § 7. Forward-Link Declaration Syntax
-- ================================================================
--
-- forward-link [name] -> [file].[form-name]
--
-- A forward-link names a dependency on a form that is not yet FORM.
-- The target may be FRAME (exists, has structure, has not yet closed).
-- The ordinal_reflection of any forward-link pair is ~~ at most
-- until the target achieves FORM (ordinal_reflection-is-unconditional, step 4).
--
-- Forward-links are required to be named explicitly.
-- An unlabeled dependency on a FRAME target is a hidden forward-link —
-- it carries the FRAME status invisibly into downstream derivations.
-- Naming it makes the provisional status visible to the kernel.
--
-- Forward-links in this file:
--   forward-link subtraction-context -> subtraction.hm.ordinatic-subtraction
--   forward-link wrap-context        -> ordinatic-wrap.hm.W-as-ordinal_reflection
--
-- These are provisional ~~. They promote when their targets close.

-- ================================================================
-- § 8. Updated presupposes Syntax
-- ================================================================
--
-- All prior .hm files use bulk "presupposes: file" headers.
-- The links.hm format replaces this with named link declarations
-- (shown in the file header above).
-- The kernel, when implemented, will use named link declarations
-- for step-level provenance tracing.
-- Bulk presupposes declarations in prior files remain valid —
-- they are ~~-level dependencies (the content is similar to named links
-- but the exact step-level targets are not yet specified).
-- When the kernel is written, bulk presupposes will be promoted
-- to named link declarations as each file is reviewed.
-- FRAME: bulk-to-named migration for axioms.hm, casting.hm, verify.hm.
